    "type": "question",
    "label": "18",
    "prompt": "A coin is biased so that for any single throw, the probability that it lands on heads is $\\frac23$. This coin is placed in a bag with two regular coins. A coin is drawn from the bag at random and tossed three times. The coin lands on heads twice. Find the probability that the biased coin was chosen.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{16}{43}$.\n\nWeight each coin type’s probability of two heads by its probability of being selected.",
      "worked": "Let $B$ mean the biased coin was chosen, $F$ mean a fair coin was chosen, and $H$ mean exactly two heads were obtained.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(H\\mid B)&=\\binom32\\left(\\frac23\\right)^2\\left(\\frac13\\right)=\\frac49\\\\\\Pr(H\\mid F)&=\\binom32\\left(\\frac12\\right)^3=\\frac38.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $\\Pr(B)=\\frac13$ and $\\Pr(F)=\\frac23$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(B\\mid H)&=\\frac{\\frac13\\times\\frac49}{\\frac13\\times\\frac49+\\frac23\\times\\frac38}\\\\\\Pr(B\\mid H)&=\\frac{\\frac4{27}}{\\frac4{27}+\\frac14}\\\\\\Pr(B\\mid H)&=\\frac{16}{43}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 42
  },
